1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,560
Come in, both of you. So, how's the
renewable energy presentation going?

2
00:00:04,899 --> 00:00:07,740
Quite well, but we've
got far too much material.

3
00:00:08,465 --> 00:00:12,920
We'd planned for 20 minutes, and then
we noticed 15 is the absolute maximum.

4
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:16,460
That's right, and anyone
who goes over gets stopped.

5
00:00:17,110 --> 00:00:18,500
Remind me how you've split it up?

6
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:24,240
I'm doing the solar side, how the panels
work and how their efficiency has improved.

7
00:00:25,235 --> 00:00:28,101
And I'm looking at wind.
Not the technology so

8
00:00:28,125 --> 00:00:31,192
much, more what wind
farms cost to build and run.

9
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:33,700
Good. How are you getting on with sources?

10
00:00:34,140 --> 00:00:36,360
We've got six academic ones so far.

11
00:00:36,760 --> 00:00:39,660
That's not enough, I'm afraid.
Eight is the minimum.

12
00:00:40,365 --> 00:00:42,400
Have you looked at
Sustainable Energy Review?

13
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,440
It's a journal, and the recent issues
have good articles on both solar and wind.

14
00:00:47,700 --> 00:00:50,820
I've used renewable energy
today, but not that one.

15
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:54,580
It's worth a look. Now, the first draft.
When's that due?

16
00:00:54,879 --> 00:00:56,000
Isn't it week five?

17
00:00:56,539 --> 00:00:59,220
It was, but they moved it
back a week, didn't they?

18
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:01,520
They did. It's week six now.

19
00:01:02,125 --> 00:01:05,260
Tom, for the wind section,
I'd bring in a real example.

20
00:01:05,814 --> 00:01:10,420
Germany's the obvious one, but Denmark
gets far more of its electricity from wind,

21
00:01:10,719 --> 00:01:12,100
so I'd use that instead.

22
00:01:12,354 --> 00:01:13,354
Good idea.

23
00:01:13,454 --> 00:01:15,240
Priya, have you found anything useful?

24
00:01:15,484 --> 00:01:19,960
Yes! A report on global solar
capacity from the International Agency.

25
00:01:20,599 --> 00:01:23,020
It's much more recent than
the government data I had.

26
00:01:23,369 --> 00:01:26,280
And Tom, how will you show
how costs have changed?

27
00:01:26,529 --> 00:01:29,753
I'd thought about a table,
but a graph shows the trend

28
00:01:29,777 --> 00:01:33,312
much better, so I'll plot the
costs over the last 20 years.

29
00:01:33,640 --> 00:01:34,640
Agreed.

30
00:01:36,529 --> 00:01:40,537
A general point about slides. People put
far too much on them. Pictures are fine.

31
00:01:40,561 --> 00:01:44,192
It's the numbers I'd keep off. All
those statistics belong in your notes.

32
00:01:44,419 --> 00:01:47,060
That's helpful.
Our slides are really crowded.

33
00:01:47,439 --> 00:01:52,880
My rule is four bullet points per
slide at the most. Six is far too many.

34
00:01:53,100 --> 00:01:54,100
We'll cut them down.

35
00:01:54,169 --> 00:01:57,263
And your reference list
needs to follow the Harvard

36
00:01:57,287 --> 00:02:00,332
style, not APA, which a
few groups used last year.

37
00:02:00,539 --> 00:02:05,197
About the rehearsal, we tried to
book room 241, but it was taken.

38
00:02:05,221 --> 00:02:07,912
So we've got 214
on the second floor.

39
00:02:08,259 --> 00:02:09,259
And when is it?

40
00:02:09,980 --> 00:02:14,600
We wanted Monday, but Sam has
a lab then, so it's Tuesday next week.

41
00:02:14,900 --> 00:02:18,480
I'll try to come along, and I'll send
you written comments within three days.

42
00:02:19,235 --> 00:02:23,520
Sam's doing the policy section, by the way.
Government incentives and regulation.

43
00:02:23,940 --> 00:02:25,560
Good! That rounds it off.

44
00:02:26,200 --> 00:02:28,774
Make sure your final
slide pulls all three parts

45
00:02:28,798 --> 00:02:31,332
together in a summary,
rather than just saying,

46
00:02:31,609 --> 00:02:32,609
Any questions?

47
00:02:32,940 --> 00:02:36,873
And remember, content isn't
everything. Part of the mark

48
00:02:36,897 --> 00:02:40,772
is for delivery. Eye contact,
pace, that kind of thing.

49
00:02:41,020 --> 00:02:42,140
We'll practice properly.

50
00:02:42,634 --> 00:02:45,900
When you do, time each section
with a timer on your phone.

51
00:02:46,230 --> 00:02:49,380
And the final deadline, is
that the end of this month?

52
00:02:50,180 --> 00:02:53,860
No, you've got a little longer.
It's the third of next month.

53
00:02:54,190 --> 00:02:56,920
Thanks very much, this
has been really helpful.

